                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "The game describes itself as relaxing take on the god game genre, and even looks like one. The gameplay however has nothing to do with a god game. It is basically the 'Simon says' segment from Keep Talking and Nobody Explodes with just enough extra mechanics to make it a 99 cents standalone game.\n\n[h1]The gameplay[/h1]\n\nYou are given a combinations of colors, and need to press the same colors in the right order on a spinning shape (planet).  This way you earn currency (awe) and unlock new colors (biomes). Pressing the wrong color in a combination does not punish you, but just makes you restart the combination. With the currency you buy the new colors to place on the shape, so you can complete more of the given combinations which require these colors. You complete a level when you have added enough patches of each color and have unlocked and bought an item that does nothing for your current shape.\nAfter the first couple of levels the combinations are not just given, but you need to figure them out. Sometimes they are backwards, flashing or both. This slowly gets a little harder each level but never requires much thinking. If all else fails you can just keep pressing colors until you get a combination right or at least part of it to understand the combination. \nLater a timer (seasons) is added which changes some of the colors on the shape, making the game just a little bit harder.\nThere are 5 chapters (solar systems you can name) with 5 levels (planets you can name) each. Plus the Christmas and Halloween chapters, also 5 levels each. This makes a total of 35 levels, and since later levels are mostly spend waiting for the correct color to pass on the spinning shape this means you can take up to several hours to complete the game.\n\n[h1]Verdict[/h1]\n\nMy biggest problem with the game is how it is advertised and what it actually offers. The store page promises puzzles, discoveries and a spiritual experience among other things. \"The player is a god in a yet-to-be-shaped universe\" it says. Unfortunately the reality is that I am just clicking colors in order, and this not why I bought this game. It may be relaxing and fun for others, but I think there is just not enough fun lements to keep you playing if you are not an achievement hunter like me.  The nice presentation can’t hide or fix that…",
